L225: ### 3.1 Pre & Mid-Training: Building a Base Model
L226: Training begins with synthetic pretraining and mid-training, leveraging available large-scale datasets to learn strong priors for shape and texture, and skills such as mask-following, occlusion handling, and pose estimation. The rich features learned here drastically reduce the number of labeled real-world samples required in post-training (cite142†Hernandez et al., 2021 ), which generally incur acquisition costs.
L227: In pre- and mid-training, models are trained with rectified conditional flow matching (cite143†Liu et al., 2022 ) to generate multiple 3D modalities (see cite74†Section C.2 ).
L228: #### 3.1.1 Pretraining: Single Isolated 3D Assets
L229: Pretraining trains the model to reconstruct accurate 3D shapes and textures from renders of isolated synthetic objects, following the successful recipes from (cite124†Xiang et al., 2025 ; cite125†Yang et al., 2024b ; cite144†Wu et al., 2024 ).
L230: Specifically, we gather a set of image $I$, shape $S$, and texture $T$ triplets, using $2.7$ million object meshes from Objaverse-XL (cite145†Deitke et al., 2023 ) and licensed datasets, and render them from $24$ viewpoints, each producing a high-resolution image of a single centered object; more detail in cite66†Section B.1 . We call this dataset Iso-3DO and train for 2.5 trillion training tokens.
L231: #### 3.1.2 Mid-Training: Semi-Synthetic Capabilities
L232: 
L233: Next, mid-training builds up foundational skills that will enable the model to handle objects in real-world images:
L234: 
L235:   * •
L236: 
L237: Mask-following: We train the model to reconstruct a target object, defined by a binary mask on the input image.
L238: 
L239:   * •
L240: 
L241: Occlusion robustness: The artificial occluders in our dataset incentivize learning shape completion.
L242: 
L243:   * •
L244: Layout estimation: We train the model to produce translation and scale in normalized camera coordinates.
L245: We construct our data by rendering textured meshes into natural images using alpha compositing. This “render-paste” dataset contains one subset of occluder-occludee pairs, and another subset where we replace real objects with synthetic objects at similar location and scale, creating physically-plausible data with accurate 3D ground truth. We call this data RP-3DO; it contains $61$ million samples with $2.8$ million unique meshes; cite146†Figure 3 shows examples. See cite67†Section B.2 for more details.
L246: After mid-training (2.7 trillion training tokens), the model has now been trained with all input and output modalities for visually grounded 3D reconstruction. However, all data used has been (semi-)synthetic; to both close the domain gap and fully leverage real-world cues, we need real images.
L247: cite147†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: Life of an example going through the data collection pipeline. We streamline annotation by breaking it into subtasks: annotators first choose target objects (Stage 1); rank and select 3D model candidates (Stage 2); then pose these models within a 2.5D scene (Stage 3). Stages 2 and 3 use model-in-the-loop.
L248: ### 3.2 Post-Training: Real-World Alignment
L249: 
L250: In post-training, we have two goals. The first is to close the domain gap between (semi-)synthetic data and natural images. The second is to align with human preference for shape quality. We adapt the model by using our data engine iteratively; we first (i) collect training data with the current model, and then (ii) update our model using multi-stage post-training on this collected data. We then repeat.
L251: #### 3.2.1 Post-Training: Collection Step
L252: The core challenge with collecting data for 3D visual grounding is that most people cannot create meshes directly; this requires skilled 3D artists, who even then can take multiple hours. This is different from the segmentation masks collected in SAM (cite148†Kirillov et al., 2023 ). However, given options, most people can choose which mesh resembles an object in the image most accurately. This fact forms the foundation of our data collection for SAM 3D.
L253: We convert preferences into training data as follows: sample from our post-trained model, ask annotators to choose the best candidate and then grade its overall quality according to a rubric which we define and update. If the quality meets the (evolving) bar, the candidate becomes a training sample.
L254: Unfortunately at the first iteration, our initial model yields few high-quality candidates. This is because before the first collection step, very little real-world data for 3D visual grounding exists. We deal with this cold start problem by leveraging a suite of existing learned and retrieval-based models to produce candidates. In early stages, we draw mostly from the ensemble, but as training progresses our best model dominates, eventually producing about $80\%$ of the annotated data seen by SAM 3D.
L255: Our annotation pipeline collects 3D object shape $S$, texture $T$, orientation $R$, 3D location $t$, and scale $s$ from real-world images. We streamline the process by dividing into subtasks and leveraging existing appropriate models and human annotators within each (see cite149†Figure 5 ): identifying target objects, 3D model ranking and selection, and posing these within a 3D scene (relative to a point map). We outline each stage of the data engine below and present details in cite47†Appendix A .
L256: In total, we annotate almost $1$ million images with $\sim 3.14$ million untextured meshes and $\sim 100$K textured meshes–unprecedented scale for 3D data paired with natural images.
L257: ##### Stage 1: Choosing target objects $(I,M)$.
L258: 
L259: The goal of this stage is to identify a large, diverse set of images $I$ and object masks $M$ to lift to 3D. To ensure generalization across objects and scenes, we sample images from several diverse real-world datasets, and utilize a 3D-oriented taxonomy to balance the object distribution. To obtain object segmentation masks, we use a combination of pre-existing annotations (cite148†Kirillov et al., 2023 ) and human labelers selecting objects of interest.
