L320: The metrics measure bounding box overlap, rotation error, and chamfer-like distances normalized by object diameter.
L321: ##### 3D shape and texture.
L322: We evaluate single-object generation by comparing SAM 3D with prior state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods. In human preference studies, SAM 3D achieves an $5:1$ head-to-head win rate on real images (see cite172†Figure 8 ). cite173†Table 2 presents quantitative evaluation on shape quality, where SAM 3D matches or exceeds previous SOTA performance on isolated object images (ISO3D), and significantly outperforms all baselines on challenging real-world inputs (SA-3DAO).
L323: Qualitative examples in cite174†Figure 6 further illustrate the model’s strong generalization under heavy occlusion. In cite175†Figure 9 , we compare SAM 3D texture vs. other texture models, given SAM 3D shapes (SAM 3D’s improved shape actually benefits other methods in this eval). Annotators significantly prefer SAM 3D texture (details in cite102†Section E.2 ).
L324: ##### 3D scene reconstruction.
L325: In preference tests on three evaluation sets, users prefer scene reconstructions from SAM 3D by $6:1$ over prior SOTA (cite172†Figure 8 ). cite176†Figure 7 and cite177†Figure 22 in the appendix shows qualitative comparisons, while cite170†Table 3 shows quantitative metrics for object layout. On real-world data like SA-3DAO and ADT, the improvement is fairly stark and persists even when pipeline approaches use SAM 3D meshes.
