L526: ## Appendix A Data Annotation Engine Details
L527: 
L528: ### A.1 Stage 1: Image and Object Candidate Sourcing
L529: ##### Image sources.
L530: To promote generalization across diverse real-world scenes, we expanded our domain coverage by sourcing images from multiple datasets.
L531: These include large-scale web-sourced imagery (SA-1B (cite148†Kirillov et al., 2023 ), MetaCLIP (cite168†Xu et al., 2024 )), video data capturing everyday environments (SA-VI (cite258†Li et al., 2023a )), egocentric video datasets (Ego4D (cite259†Grauman et al., 2022 ), Ego-Exo4D (cite260†Grauman et al., 2024 ), AEA (cite261†Lv et al., 2024 ), AEO (cite262†Straub et al., 2024 ), Nymeria (cite263†Ma et al., 2024 )), and domain-specific collections such as food (Food Recognition (cite264†Bossard et al., 2014 )) and driving scenes (BDD100k (cite265†Yu et al., 2020 )).
L532: We first filter out images with low resolution, severe blurriness, low contrast, or noticeable artifacts to ensure high-quality visual inputs that are representative of real-world scenarios. Next, we employ visual-language models for object recognition to generate object-level annotations for each image. Images containing only uninformative backgrounds (e.g., ground, sky, ocean) without salient 3D objects are subsequently removed from the dataset.
