L556: Image-to-3D generation: Image-to-3D methods, including our own SAM 3D checkpoint, generate 3D objects in the form of point clouds or meshes, conditioned on the image input. When successful, this tends to produce examples that go beyond semantic matches and better respect the object’s physical appearance in the image. However, lack of robustness to occlusion or clutter can negatively impact the results.
L557: ##### 3D texture model suite.
L558: 
L559: For texture generation, we utilize image-to-3D models, multi-view texture generation models, and our own SAM 3D checkpoints. All texture candidates are generated using shapes produced by the SAM 3D Geometry model, ensuring that texture models have the best chance of success, even in cases of heavy occlusion.
L560: 
L561: cite267†Image: Refer to caption Figure 14: Stage 2 UI sketch. Annotators can only choose between options; they cannot directly edit the meshes or textures.
L562: ##### Stage 2 Selection procedure.
L563: The annotators select the best-of-$N$ candidates by making a series of pairwise comparisons (see cite268†Figure 14 ). For each object, the annotator is initially presented with two candidates to compare and is asked to pick from the following three choices: “Left” (is better), “Right” (is better), or “Equal Quality”. Because the options are in 3D, we by default automatically rotate the objects on a turntable, but annotators are free to rotate the objects as they wish, or zoom the camera.
L564: After making a selection, the non-selected option is replaced by a new candidate; if “Equal Quality”, we randomly choose which candidate to keep. The selection procedure continues until all candidates have been shown. We randomize the order in which candidates are presented to the annotator, to prevent biases due to order from affecting the selection process.
L565: After the best candidate is identified in the selection process, annotators are asked to rate the mesh against a predefined quality bar $\alpha$. Examples meeting the bar will become candidates to enter Stage 3 for alignment, while examples under the bar will become negative examples for preference alignment or considered as candidates for manual mesh generation in Stage 2.5.
L566: ### A.3 Stage 2.5: 3D Artist Mesh Details
L567: When the 3D model-in-the-loop suite fails to generate an acceptable mesh for a particular sample, the aforementioned preference-based annotation approach is unable to provide the data needed to improve the model for such objects. To overcome this data distribution blind spot, we work with a team of 3D artists to build meshes for such hard meshes.
L568: Given the high cost of specialized 3D artists, we seek to maximize their value by ensuring each object sent to the 3D artists represents a genuine failure case that cannot be resolved by the data engine alone. To maximize the value of this investment, we develop a refined labeling framework that categorizes failures into common types: e.g., complex geometry, occlusion, transparency, and small object size. We balance sampling across these categories.
L569: In addition, we employ clustering techniques over images, 3D latents, and object semantics to deduplicate candidates, ensuring that one or a few representative samples per group suffices for effective coverage in data sampling.
L570: Additional details on the data collection process for meshes created by 3D artists can be found in cite92†Section D.1 , which employed a similar mesh creation process by the artists, but with more intentional curation of inputs.
L571: ### A.4 Stage 3: 3D Mesh Alignment
L572: We collect object pose annotations by aligning meshes from prior stages to a scene point cloud derived from the input image. To make this accessible to generalist annotators, we designed and implemented an annotation tool which allows the annotator to manipulate 3D meshes to align to a 2.5D point cloud pre-computed by an off-the-shelf depth estimator. Annotators can use either keyboard or mouse to rotate, translate, and scale the meshes so that the mesh is accurately anchored to the 2.5D point cloud.
L573: We also provide additional functions including (a) mesh visibility toggle, (b) target indicator toggle, (c) point cloud size adjustment, (d) control visibility toggle, (e) undo, (f) camera view reset and pre-defined view, and (g) mesh IOU indictator as shown in cite269†Figure 15 .
L574: cite270†Image: Refer to caption Figure 15: Stage 3 UI sketch. The UI supports annotators in directly placing the object in the 2.5D pointcloud.
L575: ### A.5 Annotation Statistics
L576: 
L577:   * •
L578: 
L579: Stage 1: Annotators on average spend 10 seconds to segment a single interesting object. We utilize SAM (cite148†Kirillov et al., 2023 ) as a tool to assist in segmentation.
L580: 
L581:   * •
L582: 
L583: Stage 2: Annotators on average spend 80 seconds to select the best candidate shape/texture from 6-10 candidate meshes from variable sources.
L584: 
L585:   * •
L586: 
L587: Stage 3: Annotators on average spend 150 seconds to anchor and orient the matched 3D shape to the 2.5D point cloud.
L588: 
L589:   * •
L590: Over the lifetime of the project (including development), our MITL data engine yields 3.14 million trainable shapes, 1.23 million samples of layout data,  100K trainable textures, and over 7 million pairwise preferences.
