L345: ##### Multi-stage training improves performance.
L346: 
L347: SAM 3D’s real-world performance emerges through multi-stage training. cite181†Table 4 reveals near-monotonic 3D shape improvements as each training stage is added, validating the approach that leads to the final model (last row). In the appendix, cite182†Figure 19 shows similar results for texture and cite183†Table 7 shows the contribution of each individual real-world data stage, by knocking out the MITL-3DO, Art-3DO data, or DPO stages.
L348: ##### Other ablations.
L349: 
L350: Please see the appendix for additional ablations on rotation representation (cite106†Section E.4 ), DPO (cite75†Section C.3 ), distillation (cite77†Section C.4 ), pointmaps (cite107†Section E.5 ), and scaling best-of-$N$ in the data engine (cite62†Section A.7 ).
L351: 
L352: ## 5 Related Work
L353: ##### 3D reconstruction
L354: has been a longstanding challenge in computer vision. Classical methods include binocular stereopsis (cite184†Wheatstone, 1838 ), structure-from-motion (cite185†Hartley and Zisserman, 2003 ; cite186†Szeliski, 2022 ; cite187†Scharstein and Szeliski, 2002 ; cite188†Torresani et al., 2008 ; cite189†Tomasi and Kanade, 1992 ), and SLAM (cite190†Smith et al., 1990 ; cite191†Castellanos et al., 1999 ).
L355: Other strategies reconstruct by analysis (e.g., silhouettes (cite192†Esteban and Schmitt, 2004 )) or by synthesis via volume rendering (cite193†Kajiya and Von Herzen, 1984 ), using either implicit representations (cite194†Mildenhall et al., 2020 ) or explicit ones (cite195†Sitzmann et al., 2019 ; cite196†Liu et al., 2020 ).
L356: Supervised deep learning methods predict voxels (cite197†Xie et al., 2019 ; cite198†Wang et al., 2021 ), point clouds (cite199†Van Hoorick et al., 2022 ), or meshes (cite200†Worchel et al., 2022 ; cite201†Wen et al., 2019 ), or optimize implicit representations (cite202†Liu et al., 2024 ), e.g., signed distance functions (SDFs), often with high-quality output but requiring multiple views at inference. In contrast, we focus on the more restrictive setting of a single RGB image at test time.
L357: ##### Single-view 3D reconstruction
L358: is considerably more difficult. A large body of work trains models with direct 3D supervision, predicting meshes (cite203†Xu et al., 2019 ; cite204†Kulkarni et al., 2022 ), voxels (cite205†Girdhar et al., 2016 ; cite206†Wu et al., 2017 ), point clouds (cite207†Fan et al., 2017 ; cite208†Mescheder et al., 2019 ), or CAD-aligned geometry (cite209†Wang et al., 2018 ; cite123†Gkioxari et al., 2019 ).
L359: A recent line of work (cite210†Zhang et al., 2023 ; cite124†Xiang et al., 2025 ; cite211†Ren et al., 2024 ) supervises with VAE (cite212†Kingma and Welling, 2013 ) latent representations. However, these methods are typically evaluated on simplified synthetic single-object benchmarks such as ShapeNet (cite213†Chang et al., 2015 ), Pix3D (cite214†Sun et al., 2018 ) or Objaverse (cite145†Deitke et al., 2023 ).

